  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B051",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B020",
    "B009"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks combine a precise physical idea—suppressing lattice-induced clock shifts at a magic wavelength—with sustained, independently reproducible performance in atomic ensembles. They have become a robust route to new tests and measurements, not simply a prospective standards technology. On the supplied record, Katori's originating concept and Ye's decisive experimental development remain the most compact and intelligible allocation of credit.",
    "case_against": "The central remaining issue is historical attribution: the citation must distinguish the foundational lattice-clock invention and realization from frequency-comb work, later metrological infrastructure, and subsequent applications. We should not infer that two names exhaust all important contributions without the documentary priority review identified by the chair.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the direct chain from the magic-wavelength concept to stable, reproducible clock performance, together with the Katori–Ye attribution, gives B051 an advantage over otherwise powerful alternatives.",
        "response": "I agree. For precision physics, repeated quantitative agreement across laboratories is particularly persuasive evidence of maturity. I also share his caution that the prize should recognize the defining physical and experimental advance, rather than claim individual credit for a future redefinition of the second or for the wider optical-clock community."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson favors squeezed light because it made quantum fluctuations an experimentally controllable resource, while recognizing that the proper experimental recipients and the boundary from later interferometer deployment require review.",
        "response": "B009 remains a very strong alternative, and its control of quantum noise is a beautiful fundamental result. But its proposed theory-to-experiment allocation and the separation from later large-scale implementation remain less sharply delimited on this record than the lattice-clock achievement. That comparison, rather than breadth of support, is why I retain B051."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson gives priority to IceCube's opening of high-energy neutrino astronomy but identifies fair individual credit in an observatory-scale collaboration as the decisive constraint.",
        "response": "I share the admiration for a new cosmic messenger channel and agree that the scientific result is compelling. Yet the attribution constraint is fundamental for a prize to individuals: the supplied record does not establish that the proposed three names can represent the detector, collaboration, and discovery fairly. B051 has comparable experimental solidity with a more defensible present allocation."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether contemporaneous priority documentation confirms that Katori and Ye alone represent all indispensable contributions to the original optical-lattice-clock concept and decisive realization.",
      "How narrowly the final citation should delimit optical lattice clocks from broader optical-clock, frequency-comb, and later precision-metrology developments."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct conflict with the proposed laureates is apparent. My longstanding collaborations in attosecond atomic physics concern other researchers and do not bear on this configuration."
  }
}
